@page
@model Web.Pages.HowItWorksModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How the Predictions Work";
    ViewData["HideBackground"] = true;
    var test = Model.Info.Test;
}

<article class="prose">
    <div class="fighter-header">
        <h1>How the predictions work</h1>
        <p class="fighter-meta">A logistic regression on each fighter's UFC history, tested on fights it never saw.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">How well it does</h2>
    <p>
        The model was trained on every UFC fight before @test.From.ToString("MMMM yyyy") and then asked to pick
        the @test.Model.Bouts.ToString("N0") fights from @test.From.ToString("MMM yyyy") to @test.To.ToString("MMM yyyy"),
        using only what was known the day before each one.
    </p>
    <div class="table-card section-card">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Picks the winner</th><th>Log loss</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>This model</strong></td><td><strong>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.Model.Accuracy)</strong></td><td><strong>@test.Model.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Whoever has the better UFC win rate</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.BetterWinRate.Accuracy)</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Whoever has the higher Elo rating</td><td>@HowItWorksModel.Percent(test.EloOnly.Accuracy)</td><td>@test.EloOnly.LogLoss?.ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Coin flip</td><td>50.0%</td><td>0.693</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        On picking winners alone, the model is no better than choosing the fighter with the better UFC
        record: MMA is hard to predict from records, and most of what they say is in the win rate. Where the model adds
        something is the probability. Log loss rewards being confident when right and punishes being confident when wrong
        (lower is better), and the model has the best score. Its percentages mean what they say:
    </p>
    <div class="table-card section-card calibration">
        @foreach (var bucket in test.Calibration)
        {
            <div class="calibration-row">
                <span class="calibration-label">Favorite given @((bucket.From * 100).ToString("0"))&ndash;@((bucket.To * 100).ToString("0"))%</span>
                <div class="calibration-bars">
                    <div class="calibration-bar predicted" style="width: @((bucket.Predicted * 100).ToString("0"))%"><span>said @((bucket.Predicted * 100).ToString("0"))%</span></div>
                    <div class="calibration-bar actual" style="width: @((bucket.Actual * 100).ToString("0"))%"><span>won @((bucket.Actual * 100).ToString("0"))%</span></div>
                </div>
                <span class="calibration-count">@bucket.Bouts fights</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">What goes into a prediction</h2>
    <p>Everything comes from the two fighters' UFC fights before the one being predicted:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Elo rating</strong>: a rating that rises with wins and falls with losses, by more when the result is an upset.</li>
        <li><strong>Record and form</strong>: UFC win rate, the last three results, and the current win or losing streak.</li>
        <li><strong>Finishing</strong>: knockouts and submissions per fight, and how often the fighter has been finished.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>UFC experience</strong>: number of UFC fights. Once record is accounted for, more fights counts slightly
            <em>against</em> a fighter, probably because long careers mean older fighters.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Time since last fight</strong>. A longer gap counts slightly in a fighter's favor, probably because
            fighters on a quick turnaround are often short-notice replacements.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Fighters making their UFC debut have no history, so they're treated as average. Dream fights use each fighter as of
        their last UFC fight.
    </p>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">What was tried and left out</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Height, reach, age, and stance</strong> made predictions worse on the years held out for choosing the model.
            The data is the likely reason: ages stopped updating on UFC.com for many retired fighters, and many reaches are missing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Career stats</strong> such as strikes landed per minute are current totals that include the very fights being
            predicted, so using them would leak the results.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Gradient boosting</strong> did no better than the logistic regression, which is easier to explain.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Betting odds</strong> aren't used. Odds reflect things no record shows, like injuries and short-notice
            replacements, so a bookmaker's favorite is a stronger pick than this model's.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="stats-section-title">Cleaning the data first</h2>
    <p>
        The first version of the model picked 71% of winners, which was too good to be true. Duplicates in the data were
        leaking results:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The API lists most recent events twice under different addresses (for example "UFC 322" and "UFC 322: Della
            Maddalena vs. Makhachev"), so a fight's own result showed up in the history used to predict its twin. The scraper
            now marks these, and the site and model skip them.
        </li>
        <li>Some fights appear twice on one card, or on an unrelated later card. These are marked the same way.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The API also lists the loser first in almost every fight, which a model could learn instead of anything real, so
        each fight is used both ways round in training.
    </p>
    <p class="fighter-meta">
        Trained on @Model.Info.TrainingBouts.ToString("N0") fights (@Model.Info.TrainedOn). Elo K-factor @Model.Info.EloK.
    </p>
</article>
